  "section8_target_theorem": {
    "corrected_normal_form": "ONE terminal normal form (the 2-edge tail: complete hub at orbit1/phase4, then R2 at ell=0), reached by TWO structurally distinct preparation families: H3-type (orbit-1 phase walking, O stays 2, 3 preparation edges) and N2-type (fresh-orbit opening via Z3, O grows to 4-5, 5 preparation edges).",
    "statement_tested": "N2 is exactly H3 with ONE zero-charge preparation block inserted (a delayed form of the same family).",
    "verdict": "\ubc18\uc99d\ub428 (falsified as literally stated)",
    "what_IS_shared": "All five states share an identical TERMINAL mechanism, which is the part that actually produces same-component: R1 targets orbit 1 (hex 0's unique residual orbit at ell=4); the hub completer -- R1 itself or a later zero-charge event -- lands specifically on (orbit 1, phase 4) = hex 0 position 5; R2 then fires immediately at ell=0 with source orbit 1 and target orbit 0. Every one of the five ends with the identical final macro-edge rot^0;w3:120, has phi=0, and has exactly 3 legal trailing edges.",
    "why": "N2 has TWO more preparation macro-edges than H3 (5 vs 3), not one. More decisively, N2's extra edges are Z3 (fresh-orbit-opening) events that H3 never uses at all: H3 keeps O=2 throughout while N2 reaches O=4 or O=5. And the hub-completer role differs: in N2 the completer is always R1 itself, while H3 contains both variants (2 states where a zero-charge Z2 completes the hub, 1 where R1 does). So N2 is not a delayed copy of H3 -- it is a structurally distinct preparation family."
  },
